 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1045 "Tangent Vectors and Tangent Lines to Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186120011 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7247ca88-471f-5303-aa77-7e868bf58346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120216 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120216 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120216 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120216 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120216 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e918f3b-d930-5ea5-9b21-8651bb576465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120216 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =c\\,⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =⟨4ct,\\,c{e}^{t},\\,2c{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} &=4ct \\\\ {e}^{t} &=c{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t} &=2c{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} & =1\\cdot 4t \\\\ {t}^{2} & =2t \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{2},\\,{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2(2)^{2},\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186120253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120253 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120253 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8969d65-ef55-525e-9adb-a40d8a396669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120253 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j]\\cdot (i+t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot t & =0 \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(4+{t}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since $4+{t}^{2}>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=0\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})\\,j=j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120522 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1694262-cdf1-5325-9fc5-66702cda46da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120522 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨{e}^{t}\\,,\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{t}+1\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{0}\\,,\\,\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}}⟩=⟨1\\,,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120913 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120913 #edb/ref 1061 45344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120913 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d2f798c-9ffb-57a0-aea4-4212795c1dcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120913 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120914 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120914 #edb/ref 1061 45345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120914 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "989791d3-66ad-598a-a711-f5c1c0da6f59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120914 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120915 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120915 #edb/ref 1061 45348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120915 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e0c03cce-2d21-571e-98fe-f72a42288e4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120915 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120916 #edb/ref 1061 45351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120916 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74ac7b94-509d-54d2-9176-80fb0b50e930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120916 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120917 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120917 #edb/ref 1061 45392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120917 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad5a98da-8ea4-5205-9db2-94f0f8b668ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120917 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121135 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\langle t+1, \\: t^2 +1\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this function, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f'(t) = \\langle 1, \\: 2t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this function and its derivative at $t=0,$ we get the following pair of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(0)  =  \\left\\langle 1, 1 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf f'(0)  =  \\left\\langle 1, 0 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nIf we view $\\mathbf{f}'(0)$ as a vector attached to the tip of the position vector $\\mathbf f(0),$ then it will be tangent to the curve $C,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/85/854d16c788836f784d739f73063354fa3ca2ad233f03cce53b99e5764449f585.png)\n\nSince $\\mathbf f'(0) \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ it serves as a direction vector for the tangent line to the curve at $\\mathbf f(0).$ The equation of this line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u) & =f(0)+u{f}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩+u⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+u,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nIn general, if a curve $C$ is parametrized by a vector function $\\mathbf f(t),$ then the vector $\\mathbf f'(t)$ gives a **tangent vector** to $C$ at $\\mathbf{f} (t),$ provided that $\\mathbf f'(t)\\neq \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nFor a fixed $t$ in the domain of $\\mathbf{f}(t),$ the equation of the corresponding tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{R}(u) = \\mathbf{f}(t) + u\\mathbf{f}'(t), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the vector $\\mathbf f'(t)$ always points in the direction that the curve $C$ is oriented. In our example above, as $t$ increases and passes through the point $(1,1),$ the curve is transversed from left to right, and the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{f}'(0)$ is also from left to right." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121135 #edb/ref 1065 7864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121135 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dbd32d96-f4fc-5f66-861c-fc1b9746be10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121135 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259911 #edb/ref 1080 "$(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{20}{9},\\frac{8}{27})$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03f4eddd-9c61-5352-a69a-1571210da725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260032 #edb/ref 1080 "$(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{5}{9})$  and $(0,0)$" #edb/r